WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.128
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level four.

2
00:00:06.428 --> 00:00:09.814
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.114 --> 00:00:15.542
Unit three: Global Security. Today you will write formally with nominalisation.

4
00:00:15.842 --> 00:00:18.506
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:22.908
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.274
National security.

7
00:00:26.283 --> 00:00:30.884
National security means the protection of a country and its people from threats.

8
00:00:31.483 --> 00:00:34.470
National security depends on more than armies.

9
00:00:35.083 --> 00:00:38.038
Your turn. Say: National security.

10
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:42.493
National security.

11
00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.994
Cybersecurity threat.

12
00:00:45.983 --> 00:00:50.792
Cybersecurity threat means a danger to computer systems and the data they hold.

13
00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:54.579
A cybersecurity threat closed the hospital network.

14
00:00:55.183 --> 00:00:58.544
Your turn. Say: Cybersecurity threat.

15
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.074
Cybersecurity threat.

16
00:01:03.817 --> 00:01:04.841
Terrorism.

17
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:10.822
Terrorism means the use of violence against civilians to spread fear for a political aim.

18
00:01:11.417 --> 00:01:15.262
Terrorism requires cooperation between intelligence services.

19
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.259
Your turn. Say: Terrorism.

20
00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.101
Terrorism.

21
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.107
Arms control.

22
00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:29.171
Arms control means agreements that limit how many weapons countries may have.

23
00:01:29.783 --> 00:01:32.665
Arms control reduced the risk of accidental war.

24
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:35.844
Your turn. Say: Arms control.

25
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.316
Arms control.

26
00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:42.794
Intelligence agency.

27
00:01:43.783 --> 00:01:48.639
Intelligence agency means an official organisation that collects secret information.

28
00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:51.772
The intelligence agency warned of the plot.

29
00:01:52.383 --> 00:01:55.779
Your turn. Say: Intelligence agency.

30
00:01:58.633 --> 00:02:00.853
Intelligence agency.

31
00:02:01.617 --> 00:02:03.210
Conflict resolution.

32
00:02:04.217 --> 00:02:08.039
Conflict resolution means the process of ending a dispute peacefully.

33
00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.509
Conflict resolution requires patient dialogue.

34
00:02:12.117 --> 00:02:15.686
Your turn. Say: Conflict resolution.

35
00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:20.115
Conflict resolution.

36
00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:21.898
Deterrence.

37
00:02:22.883 --> 00:02:26.694
Deterrence means preventing an attack by threatening a strong response.

38
00:02:27.283 --> 00:02:30.165
Deterrence only works if the threat is believable.

39
00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.147
Your turn. Say: Deterrence.

40
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.036
Deterrence.

41
00:02:37.783 --> 00:02:39.226
Crisis management.

42
00:02:40.217 --> 00:02:45.154
Crisis management means the skills and plans used to control a dangerous emergency.

43
00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:48.168
Good crisis management prevented panic.

44
00:02:48.783 --> 00:02:51.772
Your turn. Say: Crisis management.

45
00:02:54.633 --> 00:02:55.925
Crisis management.

46
00:02:56.733 --> 00:03:00.523
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:01.383 --> 00:03:08.691
Visal, a policy paper says 'The escalation of the conflict led to a rapid deterioration in security.' What is unusual about it?

48
00:03:09.783 --> 00:03:24.803
It uses nominalisation: verbs become nouns. 'The conflict escalated, so security deteriorated rapidly' becomes 'The escalation of the conflict led to a rapid deterioration in security.'

49
00:03:25.917 --> 00:03:27.382
Why do writers do that?

50
00:03:28.483 --> 00:03:44.487
Nouns pack more information into one phrase and sound formal and objective. Examples: decide → decision, detect → detection, resolve → resolution, deter → deterrence.

51
00:03:45.583 --> 00:03:46.921
Is there any risk?

52
00:03:48.017 --> 00:03:57.925
Too many nouns can make writing heavy and unclear. Use nominalisation for reports, but keep a simple verb when you need a clear, direct message.

53
00:03:59.067 --> 00:04:00.811
Part three: grammar.

54
00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:06.064
Grammar focus: Nominalisation: verb to noun phrase.

55
00:04:06.364 --> 00:04:07.505
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:08.217 --> 00:04:12.190
The expansion of intelligence agencies raised concerns about privacy.

57
00:04:13.083 --> 00:04:16.813
The failure of arms control talks increased the risk of conflict.

58
00:04:17.717 --> 00:04:21.307
Effective deterrence depends on clear and credible warnings.

59
00:04:22.267 --> 00:04:31.418
Turn a verb into a noun (decide → decision) to make writing compact and formal: The escalation of the conflict led to… not The conflict escalated, so…

60
00:04:32.183 --> 00:04:35.950
Be careful! Don't say: The decide was taken quickly.

61
00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:40.515
Say: The decision was taken quickly.

62
00:04:40.815 --> 00:04:42.617
Use the noun 'decision' after 'the'.

63
00:04:43.433 --> 00:04:46.144
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:46.933 --> 00:04:48.782
Security in a Digital Age.

65
00:04:49.082 --> 00:04:52.660
National security once meant protecting borders from armies.

66
00:04:53.667 --> 00:04:58.615
Today, the growth of cybersecurity threats and the spread of terrorism demand new thinking.

67
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:06.696
Intelligence agencies depend on the sharing of information, while arms control relies on trust and verification.

68
00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:11.963
Successful deterrence requires the clear communication of consequences.

69
00:05:12.967 --> 00:05:19.715
Careful crisis management and the early use of conflict resolution can prevent a small incident from becoming a war.

70
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.906
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:28.168
The noun form of the verb 'decide' is blank.

72
00:05:28.468 --> 00:05:32.481
A, decider. B, decisive. C, decision.

73
00:05:36.033 --> 00:05:37.928
The answer is C: decision.

74
00:05:39.083 --> 00:05:41.385
Which sentence uses nominalisation?

75
00:05:41.685 --> 00:05:48.403
A, Leaders failed to agree.. B, The failure of leaders to agree worsened the crisis.. C, Leaders agree fast..

76
00:05:51.967 --> 00:05:56.381
The answer is B: The failure of leaders to agree worsened the crisis..

77
00:05:57.517 --> 00:06:01.084
Preventing an attack by showing a strong response is called blank.

78
00:06:01.384 --> 00:06:05.339
A, deterrence. B, terrorism. C, integration.

79
00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:11.039
The answer is A: deterrence.

80
00:06:12.183 --> 00:06:14.415
The noun form of 'escalate' is blank.

81
00:06:14.715 --> 00:06:19.262
A, escalation. B, escalator. C, escalated.

82
00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.090
The answer is A: escalation.

83
00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:29.852
According to the reading, what once defined national security?

84
00:06:30.152 --> 00:06:35.918
A, protecting borders from armies. B, cyber systems. C, global trade.

85
00:06:39.467 --> 00:06:42.407
The answer is A: protecting borders from armies.

86
00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:46.670
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:47.517 --> 00:06:51.072
How has the definition of 'security' changed with new technology?

88
00:06:59.883 --> 00:07:01.291
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:01.591 --> 00:07:30.484
Security used to mean defending territory from armies. New technology has widened the definition to include cybersecurity threats, attacks on power grids and the spread of false information online. Because these dangers cross borders instantly, governments must now share intelligence, protect critical systems and cooperate with other countries as well as strengthening their military.

90
00:07:30.784 --> 00:07:32.459
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:38.963
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and nominalisation: verb to noun phrase.

92
00:07:39.263 --> 00:07:45.596
Today's value is global security awareness. Understand that peace depends on many kinds of safety.

93
00:07:45.896 --> 00:07:48.513
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:48.813 --> 00:07:51.837
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

